n the discovery, Li attempted two classification trees: 1."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a:d01", "start": 695, "end": 713, "text": "\"Tree based on the"}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a:d01", "start": 715, "end": 894, "text": "In the historical records of southwestern Taiwan, the term \"Siraya\" refers to a type of ancient Taiwanese currency used in trade and transactions among the indigenous communities."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a:d01", "start": 895, "end": 992, "text": "This currency was often made from shells or other natural materials and played a significant role"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a:d02", "start": 0, "end": 47, "text": "are ethnically and linguistically Austronesian."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a:d02", "start": 48, "end": 149, "text": "The name \"Taiwan\" (historically \"Taiouwang\", and other variants) originated from the Siraya language."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a:d02", "start": 150, "end": 327, "text": "The Austronesian language family to which Sirayan belongs includes most of the languages spoken in the western Pacific, including Polynesian, Indonesian, Filipino and Malaysian."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a:d02", "start": 328, "end": 569, "text": "Taivoan and Makatao used to be considered two communities of the Siraya but are now classified as independent indigenous peoples, based on the latest linguistic discoveries, cultural features, and the indigenous people's self-identification."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0269-34ebdecf1b4a:d02", "start": 570, "end": 702, "text": "After the port in the Siraya area of Taiwan was annexed in 1683 by Qing Dynasty China, a process of gradual acculturation led to the"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id"